## Where I got stuck / mistakes
1. Thought strip('a]b') removes 'a]b' in the exact sequence from the target text.

2. Confused the two backslash tools: r'...' vs re.escape. Thought re.escape is what stops python from treating \ as an escape.

3. Thought re.escape could rescue a mistyped non-raw '\b'. Assumed that if a user means the set {\, b} but types '\b', re.escape could fix it.

4. Didn't see why re.escape turns \s into \\s, or why \\s is not \s in regex, thought \\s and \s are the same in effect to regex engine.

5. Did not understand why a literal backslash needs two backslashes and expected print('\') to print \.


## Fix
1. Strip treats its argument as a set of characters, not a sequence. Strip('a]b') deletes any of {a, ], b} from each end, in any order until it hits a non-member (NOT every matches in the text!!! for example 'ababababa'.strip('a') returns 'bababab'. 
This set semantics is exactly why the regex uses a character class [...] (matches any one member) rather than bare letters

2. They act on different layers, opposite directions:
   a) r'...' (raw string) acts at the python parser layer that prevents python from consuming backslashes i typed.
   b) re.escape(s) acts at runtime on data, add backslashes to neutralize regex metacharacters in a variable i am inserting into the pattern.
   c) r'...' keep the backslashes i typed, re.escape adds backslashes to data, both involve \ but they solve unrelated problems.

3. '\b' is collapsed by python's parser into a single backspace char before any re function runs. The \ and b no longer exist in the string, so re.escape (which acts later) can't recover them. The fix belongs at the input layer: write r'\b' or '\\b'. Key asymmetry: '\s' survives Python (not a valid Python escape) so re.escape can protect it; '\b' doesn't survive, so it can't.

4. They are different to the engine: \s = whitespace metacharacter; \\s = literal backslash + literal s. re.escape deliberately produces \\s so the engine reads a literal backslash (its own rule: \\ → one literal \) instead of the whitespace metacharacter.

5. '\' is not a character, it is an operator meaning "treat the next char specially".
So print('\') is a SyntaxError (\' escapes the closing quote → unterminated string). To get one literal backslash you write \\ (print('\\') → \). This rule holds independently in both layers — Python's string parser and the regex engine — which is why backslashes appear to multiply as a string passes from one to the next.